# Content assertions and lineage

Download this `.livemd` file, open it in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev), and choose
**Open → Import from file**, then **Run all**. Use Elixir 1.18 or later and OTP 27 or later
with access to public Hex. No secrets or external services are needed.

Hash exact document bytes, verify two signed generations, and check their pairwise lineage.
Refuse changed content, expiry, reversed lineage, and another profile.

The host chooses trusted attestors, content semantics, and the semantic profile digest.
Pairwise consistency does not establish the latest generation: durable high-water state,
current trust, and replay handling stay with the host. No processes or files are created.

[Open the original 1.0.0 release notebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fv1.0.0%2Fdocs%2Flivebooks%2Fbap-walkthrough.livemd).
That immutable release has the earlier walkthrough; import this file for the steps below.

## Setup

Install the exact published package. Fixed evaluation times keep the results reproducible;
only safe semantic values are persisted, never keys, signatures, tokens, or nonces.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.0.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Generate the content attestor key

This standalone profile needs one attestor key, not a grant or holder proof. Keep its private
half outside all protocol calls.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.ContentAssertion.V1, as: ContentAssertionV1
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.HistoricalPublicKey

{content_attestor_public, content_attestor_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)

now = 1_800_001_000
:attestor_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:attestor_ready
```

## Hash exact content and pin the profile

The content digest includes every supplied byte. The separate semantic-profile digest pins
the schema the host selected; the package neither fetches nor interprets that schema.

```elixir
content_bytes = ~s({"document":"example","revision":1})
{:ok, content_digest} = ContentAssertionV1.content_digest(content_bytes, %{})

# The semantic profile's identity is a caller-pinned opaque 32-byte digest:
profile_digest = :crypto.hash(:sha256, "urn:example:profile:document/1 schema bytes")

%{
  content_digest_bytes: byte_size(content_digest),
  profile_digest_bytes: byte_size(profile_digest)
}
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{content_digest_bytes: 32, profile_digest_bytes: 32}
```

## Build and sign generation one

Generation one uses the all-zero predecessor digest. Its signed context binds issuer, audience,
lineage subject, profile, content digest, and validity window.

```elixir
assertion = %ContentAssertionV1.ContentAssertion{
  attestor_key_id: "content-attestor-1",
  jti: "urn:example:assertion:2026-001",
  iss: "urn:example:issuer",
  aud: "urn:example:audience",
  sub: "urn:example:lineage:document-1",
  profile: "urn:example:profile:document/1",
  profile_digest: profile_digest,
  content_digest: content_digest,
  gen: 1,
  prev: <<0::256>>,
  iat: now,
  nbf: now,
  exp: now + 3_600
}

{:ok, assertion_input} = ContentAssertionV1.assertion_signing_input(assertion, %{})

assertion_signature =
  :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, assertion_input.message, [content_attestor_private, :ed25519])

{:ok, assertion_compact} =
  ContentAssertionV1.assemble_compact(assertion_input, assertion_signature)

:generation_one_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:generation_one_signed
```

## Verify generation one

Supply the expected content digest and context from host-owned inputs. The attestor validity
window must contain the whole assertion window.

```elixir
expected_assertion = %ContentAssertionV1.ExpectedContentAssertion{
  attestor: %HistoricalPublicKey{
    key_id: "content-attestor-1",
    public_key: content_attestor_public,
    valid_from: now - 600,
    valid_before: now + 7_200
  },
  issuer: "urn:example:issuer",
  audience: "urn:example:audience",
  subject: "urn:example:lineage:document-1",
  profile: "urn:example:profile:document/1",
  profile_digest: profile_digest,
  content_digest: content_digest,
  now: now + 60,
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, assertion_facts} =
  ContentAssertionV1.verify_assertion(assertion_compact, expected_assertion)

Map.take(assertion_facts, [:attestor_key_id, :sub, :gen, :verification, :trust])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{attestor_key_id: "content-attestor-1", sub: "urn:example:lineage:document-1", gen: 1, verification: :signature_and_window, trust: :not_evaluated}
```

## Build and sign generation two

Hash the revised document and name generation one by the digest of its exact signed compact
bytes. A changed document requires its own expected content digest.

```elixir
successor_bytes = ~s({"document":"example","revision":2})
{:ok, successor_digest} = ContentAssertionV1.content_digest(successor_bytes, %{})

successor = %{
  assertion
  | jti: "urn:example:assertion:2026-002",
    gen: 2,
    content_digest: successor_digest,
    prev: assertion_facts.digest,
    iat: now + 120,
    nbf: now + 120
}

{:ok, successor_input} = ContentAssertionV1.assertion_signing_input(successor, %{})

successor_signature =
  :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, successor_input.message, [content_attestor_private, :ed25519])

{:ok, successor_compact} =
  ContentAssertionV1.assemble_compact(successor_input, successor_signature)

:generation_two_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:generation_two_signed
```

## Verify generation two

Verify the successor signature, revised content digest, and complete context before comparing
lineage. Merely decoding a generation would not establish these facts.

```elixir
{:ok, successor_facts} =
  ContentAssertionV1.verify_assertion(successor_compact, %{
    expected_assertion
    | content_digest: successor_digest,
      now: now + 180
  })

Map.take(successor_facts, [:sub, :gen, :verification, :trust])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{sub: "urn:example:lineage:document-1", gen: 2, verification: :signature_and_window, trust: :not_evaluated}
```

## Check the successor relationship

The pairwise check requires the same lineage context, a forward generation, and the exact
predecessor digest. It returns `:ok`, not a new facts value or an authority decision.

```elixir
:ok = ContentAssertionV1.verify_successor(assertion_facts, successor_facts, %{})
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Refuse changed content and expiry

A trailing space changes the content digest even if a parser would ignore it. Evaluation
exactly at expiry also fails.

```elixir
{:ok, changed_digest} = ContentAssertionV1.content_digest(content_bytes <> " ", %{})

{:error, :invalid} =
  ContentAssertionV1.verify_assertion(assertion_compact, %{
    expected_assertion
    | content_digest: changed_digest
  })

{:error, :invalid} =
  ContentAssertionV1.verify_assertion(assertion_compact, %{
    expected_assertion
    | now: now + 3_600
  })

[:changed_content, :expired]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:changed_content, :expired]
```

## Refuse reversed lineage and cross-profile bytes

Both generations were verified, but their order cannot be reversed. A content assertion also
cannot be decoded as a role attestation or a grant.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} = ContentAssertionV1.verify_successor(successor_facts, assertion_facts, %{})

{:error, :invalid} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.RoleAttestation.V1.decode_attestation(assertion_compact, %{})

{:error, :invalid} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.decode_grant(assertion_compact, %{})

[:reversed_lineage, :role_profile, :grant_profile]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:reversed_lineage, :role_profile, :grant_profile]
```

See [operations](../guides/operations.md) for durable lineage state. An assertion proves a
trusted key signed the expected bytes and context; it does not prove the content is true.
